course: dictionary
lesson: 14421
title: Overshadow: when one thing hides another
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To overshadow something is to make it seem less important, often because something bigger or more noticeable stands near it. Think of a shadow: when a tall tree blocks the sun, the garden goes dark. «The tall tree overshadowed the little garden.»
explain: People can overshadow people too. «Her big brother is a famous footballer, and he overshadows her, even though she plays well too.» Her good play still happens, but everyone notices him more, so she seems less important.
explain: Overshadow can also make a happy time sadder. «The rain overshadowed the party.» The party still happened, but the rain spoiled the fun. Look at the words around it for clues: the rain is the bad thing that spoils the good thing.
fact: Overshadow is a verb, so its forms are overshadow, overshadows, overshadowed and overshadowing.
fact: «Overshadow» means to make something seem less important, by being more successful or noticeable.
fact: «Overshadow» can also mean to cast a shadow over something, or to tower over it.
fact: «Overshadowed» can mean a happy time was made sadder, as in «the rain overshadowed the party».
fact: If something bigger or more noticeable is next to something else, overshadow is often the right meaning.
exercise: «The tall tree overshadowed the little garden.» What does overshadowed mean here? | Watered the garden every day | Painted the garden bright green | Moved the garden to a new place | *Cast a shadow over the garden, making it seem smaller
exercise: Lily says, «The rain overshadowed the party.» What did the rain do to the party? | Made it shorter | Made it louder | *Made it sadder | Made it bigger
exercise: Copy the word that shows what the loud laugh did to the other voices in «His loud laugh overshadowed everyone else's voices.» | laugh | *overshadowed | loud | voices
test: «The new school overshadowed our old one, because it had a pool and a big garden.» What does overshadowed mean? | *Made our old school seem less special | Painted our old school | Moved our old school across town | Closed our old school for good
test: Which meaning fits overshadow when a bad event spoils a good day? | The good day was very short | *The bad news made the good day sadder | The good day made the bad news funnier | The bad news was printed in a new book
exam: In «Her brother's fame overshadowed her hard work», what does overshadowed mean? | Washed her hard work away | Made her hard work faster | *Made her hard work seem less important | Helped her hard work grow
